1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,473
AI coding agents build on open source every day.

2
00:00:02,473 --> 00:00:05,925
The people maintaining that foundation often see none of the value.

3
00:00:05,925 --> 00:00:10,665
Nebraska connects what an agent actually uses to a monthly payment for the
people behind it.

4
00:00:10,665 --> 00:00:13,396
This demonstration executes our working local system.

5
00:00:13,396 --> 00:00:17,930
All funding, identities, model decisions and transactions here are
explicitly simulated.

6
00:00:20,430 --> 00:00:24,643
A company chooses a monthly budget through a quantity based PayPal
subscription.

7
00:00:24,643 --> 00:00:25,855
One unit is one dollar.

8
00:00:25,855 --> 00:00:30,595
Nebraska only allocates money after a verified completed payment, never an
unpaid promise.

9
00:00:30,595 --> 00:00:32,280
Changes require renewed consent.

10
00:00:32,280 --> 00:00:36,178
Here, the demo organization has five hundred dollars of confirmed funding.

11
00:00:36,178 --> 00:00:41,234
Separate currency pools support dollars, euros, pounds, Canadian dollars
and Australian dollars.

12
00:00:43,734 --> 00:00:49,594
Hooks, continuous integration and the MCP server record package coordinates
and salted repository hashes.

13
00:00:49,594 --> 00:00:51,883
Source code stays on the payer’s machine.

14
00:00:51,883 --> 00:00:55,455
Observations are deduplicated per repository, package and month.

15
00:00:55,455 --> 00:00:59,306
Direct usage counts as one; each dependency level halves that amount.

16
00:00:59,306 --> 00:01:04,162
The meter supports npm, Python, Rust and Go, plus standard software bills
of materials.

17
00:01:06,662 --> 00:01:11,128
The decision model answers fixed, typed questions about criticality and
resource need.

18
00:01:11,128 --> 00:01:12,583
Its scores change the money.

19
00:01:12,583 --> 00:01:18,711
In the actual demo run, the small infrastructure package receives one
hundred sixty one dollars and twenty nine cents.

20
00:01:18,711 --> 00:01:21,256
An uncertain model falls back to neutral weights.

21
00:01:21,256 --> 00:01:23,749
A malicious README is included in the seed data.

22
00:01:23,749 --> 00:01:27,437
It cannot add a recipient, change the budget or escape the package cap.

23
00:01:29,937 --> 00:01:33,746
The treasurer initiates settlement through seven code enforced safeguards.

24
00:01:33,746 --> 00:01:36,988
Recipients must be verified and past the fourteen day cooldown.

25
00:01:36,988 --> 00:01:42,341
Funding, caps, fixed batch identity, decision types, human approval and
reproducibility are all checked.

26
00:01:42,341 --> 00:01:44,400
The suspicious fourth recipient is held.

27
00:01:44,400 --> 00:01:46,922
An emergency grant needs separate human approval.

28
00:01:46,922 --> 00:01:52,790
In this simulation, the approved two hundred fifty dollar grant is captured
and added to the maintainer’s balance.

29
00:01:55,290 --> 00:01:58,142
These are results from executing the real demo command.

30
00:01:58,142 --> 00:02:01,304
Three eligible recipients are paid by the simulated provider.

31
00:02:01,304 --> 00:02:07,474
The infrastructure maintainer receives four hundred eleven dollars and
twenty nine cents, including the separate grant.

32
00:02:07,474 --> 00:02:10,119
Running settlement again reuses the existing batch.

33
00:02:10,119 --> 00:02:12,970
In sandbox mode, the same service calls PayPal Payouts.

34
00:02:12,970 --> 00:02:17,118
Webhooks are signature verified and individual statuses are fetched from
PayPal.

35
00:02:19,618 --> 00:02:24,516
Both sides receive statements with frozen snapshots, model versions and
recompute hashes.

36
00:02:24,516 --> 00:02:28,643
Maintainers see usage counts while company names remain private by default.

37
00:02:28,643 --> 00:02:30,184
Small balances roll forward.

38
00:02:30,184 --> 00:02:35,741
Multiple maintainers can set custom shares, and verified fiscal hosts can
receive authorized payouts.

39
00:02:35,741 --> 00:02:37,887
Nebraska cannot force a company to pay.

40
00:02:37,887 --> 00:02:42,565
It removes two excuses: not knowing who to support, and making payment too
difficult.

41
00:02:42,565 --> 00:02:44,546
The AI decides where the money goes.

42
00:02:44,546 --> 00:02:46,472
Code decides where it can never go.
